The '''Ministry of the Environment''' ({{lang-ja|環境省}}, {{Nihongo|Kankyō-shō|かんきょうしょう}}) is a [[Cabinet of Okaiken|cabinet-level ministry]] of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for the formulation and implementation of [[environmental policy]], [[pollution control]], [[nature conservation]], [[climate change]] mitigation and adaptation, and the promotion of a [[circular economy]]. It is the newest of Okaiken's eleven line ministries, having been elevated from the Environment Agency to full ministerial status in the [[2001 Central Government Reform]].

The ministry oversees an annual budget of approximately {{currency|16 billion|SEN}} (roughly 1% of total government expenditure) and employs approximately 11,400 personnel across its headquarters in [[Okami]] and regional offices throughout Okaiken. Its portfolio includes the [[Environmental Impact Assessment Agency (Okaiken)|Environmental Impact Assessment Agency]], the [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]] (OUMC), and the [[National Institute for Environmental Studies (Okaiken)|National Institute for Environmental Studies]] (NIES).

The current Minister of the Environment is [[Yumi Yamada]] of the [[Social Civic Party (Okaiken)|Social Civic Party]] (SCP), who assumed office on 20 May 2026 as part of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. Her predecessor was [[Hirotaka Ishihara]] of the [[People's Party of Okaiken|People's Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), who served from November 2025 to May 2026 under the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]].

==History==

===Environment Agency era (1971–2001)===
Prior to 2001, environmental governance in Okaiken was the responsibility of the {{Nihongo|Environment Agency|環境庁|Kankyō-chō}}, established in 1971 as an external bureau of the [[Prime Minister's Office (Okaiken)|Prime Minister's Office]]. The agency was created in response to the severe pollution crises of the 1960s and early 1970s — collectively known as the {{Nihongo|"Four Pollution Diseases"|四大公害病|Shi Dai Kōgai-byō}} — which had devastated communities in industrial regions and generated widespread public outrage.

The agency's founding director-general, {{Nihongo|Bunmei Ōta|大田文明|Ōta Bunmei}}, oversaw the passage of the landmark [[Environmental Impact Assessment Act (Okaiken)|Environmental Impact Assessment Act]] of 1973 and the establishment of the nation's first comprehensive emission standards. However, the agency's status as an external bureau rather than a full ministry severely limited its bureaucratic influence. Within the Sanese administrative hierarchy, the agency was often overshadowed by the more powerful [[Ministry of International Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|Ministry of International Trade and Industry]] (MITI) and the [[Ministry of Construction (Okaiken)|Ministry of Construction]], which frequently prioritised economic growth over environmental protection.

During the 1980s and 1990s, the agency's remit expanded to include international environmental cooperation, [[ozone layer]] protection, and the regulation of [[hazardous waste]]. The passage of the {{Nihongo|[[Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act (Okaiken)|Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act]]|地熱温泉共存法|Chinetsu Onsen Kyōzon-hō}} in 1994 was a landmark achievement, establishing a legal framework for balancing [[geothermal energy]] extraction with the preservation of [[onsen|hot spring]] resources — a tension that remains one of Okaiken's most persistent environmental conflicts.

===2001 Central Government Reform===
{{Main|2001 Central Government Reform (Okaiken)}}
The [[2001 Central Government Reform]] ({{Nihongo|中央省庁再編|Chūō Shōchō Saihen}}) elevated the Environment Agency to full ministerial status as the Ministry of the Environment ({{Nihongo|環境省|Kankyō-shō}}). The reform, which was the most significant restructuring of the Sanese bureaucracy since the post-war occupation, gave the ministry independent policy-making authority, its own budgetary line, and a seat at the cabinet table.

The elevation was widely seen as a response to growing public awareness of environmental issues and the inadequacy of the agency's bureaucratic position to address complex, cross-cutting challenges such as [[climate change]], [[biodiversity]] loss, and [[waste management]]. The reform also transferred responsibility for [[nature conservation]] and [[national parks]] from the [[Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries (Okaiken)|Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries]] and the [[Ministry of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism (Okaiken)|Ministry of Construction]] to the new ministry, consolidating environmental governance under a single institutional umbrella.

However, critics noted that the ministry's budget — approximately {{currency|8 billion|SEN}} at its founding — was far smaller than those of the established ministries, and that its ability to influence policy in areas such as energy, trade, and transportation remained constrained by the enduring power of the economic ministries.

===Modern era (2001–present)===
The 21st century has seen the ministry's role expand significantly in response to the growing urgency of climate change and the evolution of Okaiken's energy landscape. The ministry played a central role in the formulation of the [[Carbon Neutrality by 2050 (Okaiken)|Carbon Neutrality by 2050]] mandate, adopted in 2020, and has overseen the development of the {{Nihongo|"Hydrogen Highway"|水素ハイウェイ|Suiso Haiwei}} initiative to promote [[hydrogen fuel]] infrastructure across the country.

The ministry has also been at the centre of several contentious policy debates, including the conflict between [[geothermal energy]] development and [[onsen]] preservation, the environmental implications of [[deep-sea mining]] in the [[Shendan Ocean]], the protection of [[Kajika people|Kajika indigenous]] fishing rights in the [[Ogasawara]] Exclusive Economic Zone, and the management of [[nuclear waste]] from Okaiken's four nuclear power plants. The [[En Crisis (2026)|En Crisis]] of January–February 2026 and the [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026 have further tested the ministry's capacity, with the former driving up energy import costs and the latter disrupting environmental monitoring systems nationwide.

==Organization==

The Ministry of the Environment is organised into internal bureaus, affiliated agencies, and special institutions. As of 2026, the ministry employs approximately 11,400 personnel across all its divisions.

===Internal bureaus===

;Minister's Secretariat ({{Nihongo|大臣官房|Daijin Kanbō}})
:The Minister's Secretariat coordinates the ministry's overall operations, manages personnel, handles public relations, and serves as the primary interface between the ministry and the Cabinet. It also oversees the ministry's internal audit functions, information security protocols, and international cooperation activities.

;Environmental Policy Bureau ({{Nihongo|環境政策局|Kankyō Seisaku-kyoku}})
:The Environmental Policy Bureau is the ministry's primary policy-formulation body, responsible for developing comprehensive environmental strategies, setting emission standards, and coordinating climate change policy across government. It oversees the implementation of the [[Carbon Neutrality by 2050 (Okaiken)|Carbon Neutrality by 2050]] mandate and manages the {{Nihongo|"Hydrogen Highway"|水素ハイウェイ|Suiso Haiwei}} initiative. The bureau also coordinates Okaiken's participation in international climate agreements and represents the ministry in inter-ministerial negotiations on energy policy.

;Nature Conservation Bureau ({{Nihongo|自然環境局|Shizen Kankyō-kyoku}})
:The Nature Conservation Bureau is responsible for the protection and management of Okaiken's [[biodiversity]], [[national parks]], [[wildlife]], and [[natural monuments]]. It administers the [[National Park system (Okaiken)|National Park system]], oversees species protection programmes, and coordinates with local governments on habitat conservation. The bureau also manages the ministry's responsibilities under the [[Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act (Okaiken)|Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act]], balancing energy extraction with the preservation of [[onsen|hot spring]] ecosystems.

;Environmental Management Bureau ({{Nihongo|環境管理局|Kankyō Kanri-kyoku}})
:The Environmental Management Bureau oversees pollution control, waste management, and environmental health. It sets and enforces air quality standards, water quality standards, and soil contamination thresholds. The bureau administers the [[Environmental Impact Assessment Act (Okaiken)|Environmental Impact Assessment Act]] and reviews environmental impact statements for major development projects. It also coordinates the ministry's circular economy initiatives, including oversight of the [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]] (OUMC).

;Global Environment Bureau ({{Nihongo|地球環境局|Chikyū Kankyō-kyoku}})
:The Global Environment Bureau handles international environmental affairs, including climate change negotiations, [[ozone layer]] protection, and transboundary pollution issues. It coordinates Okaiken's contributions to international environmental funds and manages bilateral environmental cooperation agreements. The bureau also monitors global environmental trends and provides analytical support for the ministry's international policy positions.

;Circular Economy Promotion Office ({{Nihongo|循環型社会推進室|Junkan-gata Shakai Suishin-shitsu}})
:Established in 2018, the Circular Economy Promotion Office coordinates cross-ministry efforts to promote [[waste reduction]], [[recycling]], and [[resource efficiency]]. It oversees the implementation of the [[Circular Economy Promotion Act (Okaiken)|Circular Economy Promotion Act]] of 2019 and works with the [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]] to develop urban mining and [[rare earth element]] recovery programmes.

==Key agencies==

===Environmental Impact Assessment Agency===
{{Main|Environmental Impact Assessment Agency (Okaiken)}}
The '''Environmental Impact Assessment Agency''' (EIAA; {{Nihongo|環境影響評価庁|Kankyō Eikyō Hyōka-chō}}) is an external bureau of the ministry responsible for conducting and reviewing environmental impact assessments (EIAs) for major development projects, including infrastructure construction, industrial facilities, and energy projects. Established as part of the 2001 reform, the agency has the authority to require modifications to or reject projects that fail to meet environmental standards. The agency reviews approximately 200 EIA statements annually and maintains a public database of environmental impact data.

===Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation===
{{Main|Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation}}
The '''Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation''' (OUMC; {{Nihongo|沖軒都市鉱山株式会社|Okaiken Toshi Kōzan Kabushiki-gaisha}}) is a government-affiliated corporation under the ministry's jurisdiction that promotes the recovery of valuable materials — including [[rare earth elements]], [[precious metals]], and [[critical minerals]] — from electronic waste, industrial byproducts, and end-of-life products. Established in 2015, the OUMC operates processing facilities in [[Okami]], [[Sakai]], and [[Aizawa Prefecture|Aizawa]] and has been instrumental in reducing Okaiken's dependence on imported rare earth elements. The corporation processed approximately 280,000 tonnes of e-waste in FY 2024, recovering an estimated {{currency|42 billion|SEN}} in raw materials.

===National Institute for Environmental Studies===
{{Main|National Institute for Environmental Studies (Okaiken)}}
The '''National Institute for Environmental Studies''' (NIES; {{Nihongo|国立環境研究所|Kokuritsu Kankyō Kenkyūjo}}) is the ministry's primary research institution, conducting scientific research on environmental phenomena, developing environmental monitoring technologies, and providing evidence-based policy recommendations. Founded in 1974 as the Environment Agency's research arm, the NIES was transferred to the ministry in 2001. It operates a network of 85 environmental monitoring stations across Okaiken and maintains the national environmental database. The NIES employs approximately 700 researchers and support staff.

==Key policy areas==

===Climate change and energy===
The ministry is the lead government body for climate change policy, overseeing Okaiken's commitments under international climate agreements and coordinating the implementation of the [[Carbon Neutrality by 2050 (Okaiken)|Carbon Neutrality by 2050]] mandate. The mandate, adopted in 2020, commits Okaiken to achieving [[net-zero greenhouse gas emissions]] by 2050, with an interim target of a 46% reduction from 2013 levels by 2030.

Okaiken's current energy mix target — 40% [[fossil fuel]]s, 35% [[nuclear power]], and 25% [[renewable energy]] — reflects the complex political and economic constraints facing the ministry. While the ministry advocates for an accelerated transition to renewables, it must contend with the economic realities of Okaiken's energy dependency and the political influence of the nuclear and fossil fuel industries. The {{Nihongo|"Hydrogen Highway"|水素ハイウェイ|Suiso Haiwei}} initiative, launched in 2022, aims to establish a nationwide network of [[hydrogen refuelling station]]s and promote the use of [[fuel cell vehicle]]s, with a target of 1,000 hydrogen stations by 2030.

The ministry's climate policy is implemented through a combination of regulatory measures, including [[carbon pricing]], [[emission trading scheme]]s, and [[renewable energy subsidies]]. The [[Carbon Neutrality Act (Okaiken)|Carbon Neutrality Act]] of 2021 provides the legal framework for these measures and establishes the ministerial coordination mechanisms necessary for cross-government climate action.

===Pollution control===
The ministry sets and enforces standards for [[air quality]], [[water quality]], [[soil contamination]], and [[noise pollution]]. The Environmental Management Bureau conducts regular inspections of industrial facilities and has the authority to issue compliance orders, levy fines, and recommend criminal prosecution for serious violations. The ministry also operates a nationwide pollution monitoring network and maintains a public database of emission data.

Okaiken's pollution control regime has been significantly strengthened since the environmental crises of the 1960s and 1970s, but challenges remain. [[Particulate matter]] (PM2.5) levels in [[Okami]] and other major cities frequently exceed [[World Health Organization]] guidelines, and [[chemical pollution]] from industrial estates continues to affect water quality in several prefectures.

===Nature conservation===
The Nature Conservation Bureau administers Okaiken's system of 34 [[national parks]], 56 [[quasi-national parks]], and over 300 [[prefectural natural parks]], which together cover approximately 15% of the national territory. The bureau also oversees species protection programmes for endangered flora and fauna, including the [[Sanese crested ibis]] ({{Nihongo|トキ|toki}}), the [[Sanese giant salamander]] ({{Nihongo|オオサンショウウオ|ōsanshōuo}}), and the [[Ogasawara fruit bat]].

The ministry's nature conservation work is frequently in tension with development interests, particularly in coastal and mountainous areas where tourism, agriculture, and infrastructure projects compete with ecological protection. The [[Kajika people|Kajika indigenous people]]'s traditional fishing rights in the [[Ogasawara]] EEZ represent a particularly complex conservation challenge, as the ministry must balance the protection of marine ecosystems with the recognition of indigenous rights and the pressures of commercial fishing.

===Circular economy and waste management===
The ministry has been a leading advocate for the transition to a [[circular economy]], promoting policies to reduce waste, increase recycling, and recover valuable materials from discarded products. The [[Circular Economy Promotion Act (Okaiken)|Circular Economy Promotion Act]] of 2019 established a comprehensive legal framework for waste reduction and resource efficiency, setting targets for recycling rates and mandating [[extended producer responsibility]] for electronic goods and vehicles.

The [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]] (OUMC) is the ministry's flagship circular economy initiative, recovering rare earth elements and other critical minerals from electronic waste. The ministry has also promoted the concept of {{Nihongo|"mottainai"|もったいない}} — a cultural ethic of waste avoidance — as a framework for public engagement with circular economy principles.

Okaiken's recycling rate for municipal solid waste stands at approximately 21%, below the ministry's target of 30% by 2030. The ministry has identified the construction and food service sectors as priority areas for waste reduction.

==Key legislation==

The Ministry of the Environment administers and enforces numerous statutes governing environmental protection, pollution control, and nature conservation. Key legislation includes:

* '''[[Environmental Impact Assessment Act (Okaiken)|Environmental Impact Assessment Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|環境影響評価法|Kankyō Eikyō Hyōka-hō}}, 1973): Requires environmental impact assessments for all major development projects, including infrastructure, industrial facilities, and energy projects. Establishes the procedures for EIA preparation, public consultation, and ministerial review. Significantly amended in 2001 and 2017.
* '''[[Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act (Okaiken)|Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|地熱温泉共存法|Chinetsu Onsen Kyōzon-hō}}, 1994): Establishes a legal framework for balancing [[geothermal energy]] extraction with the preservation of [[onsen|hot spring]] resources. Requires geothermal developers to conduct hydrological impact assessments and maintain buffer zones around designated onsen areas. Creates a mediation mechanism for disputes between energy companies and onsen operators.
* '''[[Carbon Neutrality Act (Okaiken)|Carbon Neutrality Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|カーボンニュートラル法|Kābon Nyūtoraru-hō}}, 2021): Provides the legal framework for Okaiken's [[Carbon Neutrality by 2050 (Okaiken)|Carbon Neutrality by 2050]] mandate. Establishes emission reduction targets, creates the ministerial coordination mechanisms for cross-government climate action, and authorises the implementation of [[carbon pricing]] and [[emission trading scheme]]s.
* '''[[Circular Economy Promotion Act (Okaiken)|Circular Economy Promotion Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|循環型社会形成推進法|Junkan-gata Shakai Keisei Suishin-hō}}, 2019): Sets comprehensive targets for waste reduction, recycling, and resource efficiency. Mandates [[extended producer responsibility]] for electronic goods, vehicles, and packaging. Establishes the legal basis for the [[Okaiken Urban Mining Corporation]].
* '''[[Nature Conservation Act (Okaiken)|Nature Conservation Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|自然環境保全法|Shizen Kankyō Hozen-hō}}, 1972): Establishes the system of national parks, quasi-national parks, and prefectural natural parks. Provides for the designation of [[wildlife protection areas]] and [[natural monuments]]. Regulates development activities within protected areas.
* '''[[Pollution Control Act (Okaiken)|Pollution Control Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|公害対策基本法|Kōgai Taisaku Kihon-hō}}, 1967, extensively amended 1993): The foundational statute of Okaiken's pollution control regime, establishing emission standards, environmental quality standards, and the liability regime for pollution damage. Originally enacted in response to the {{Nihongo|"Four Pollution Diseases"|四大公害病|Shi Dai Kōgai-byō}} crises.
* '''[[Ogasawara Marine Protection Act (Okaiken)|Ogasawara Marine Protection Act]]''' ({{Nihongo|小笠原海洋保護法|Ogasawara Kaiyō Hogo-hō}}, 2008): Establishes marine protected areas in the [[Ogasawara]] EEZ and regulates fishing activities to protect marine biodiversity. Includes provisions for the recognition of [[Kajika people|Kajika indigenous]] traditional fishing rights, subject to conservation requirements.

==Recent events==

===The En Crisis (January–February 2026)===
{{Main|En Crisis (2026)}}
The [[En Crisis (2026)|En Crisis]] of January–February 2026, in which the [[Sanese En|En]] depreciated by approximately 20% against the [[TALOD Reserve Unit|TRU]], had significant implications for the Ministry of the Environment. The sharp depreciation drove up the cost of imported [[liquefied natural gas]] (LNG), [[coal]], and other energy commodities, increasing Okaiken's energy import bill by an estimated {{currency|180 billion|SEN}} in the first quarter of 2026 alone. The ministry warned that the crisis could slow the transition to renewable energy, as higher energy costs created political pressure to maximise output from existing fossil fuel and nuclear facilities.

Environment Minister [[Hirotaka Ishihara]] convened an emergency meeting of the ministry's Energy-Environment Coordination Committee on 28 January 2026, urging accelerated deployment of solar and wind capacity to reduce exposure to imported fuel price volatility. However, the ministry's budget constraints — its {{currency|16 billion|SEN}} allocation represented approximately 1% of total government expenditure — limited its ability to fund large-scale renewable energy subsidies independently.

===The Satori AI Blackout (May 2026)===
{{Main|Satori AI Blackout}}
The [[Satori AI Blackout]] of May 2026, a catastrophic failure of the [[Satori AI]] system that caused widespread disruption to government services and infrastructure, had direct implications for the ministry's environmental monitoring operations. The NIES network of 85 environmental monitoring stations was offline for approximately 72 hours, creating a gap in air quality, water quality, and radiation monitoring data. The ministry activated backup monitoring protocols, but the incident exposed the vulnerability of Okaiken's environmental surveillance infrastructure to digital system failures.

Following the Blackout, Minister-designate [[Yumi Yamada]] announced a comprehensive review of the ministry's digital infrastructure dependencies and pledged to establish a fully independent backup monitoring system that could operate without reliance on the Satori AI platform. The ministry also raised concerns about the environmental implications of the increased data centre energy consumption associated with AI systems, calling for a formal assessment of the carbon footprint of Okaiken's digital infrastructure.

===Climate commitments and the 2026 transition===
The formation of the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]] in May 2026 brought a significant shift in the ministry's political direction. The [[Social Civic Party (Okaiken)|SCP]]-led government signalled a more ambitious approach to climate policy, with Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]] describing the Carbon Neutrality by 2050 mandate as "non-negotiable" and calling for an acceleration of the renewable energy transition. Minister Yamada, in her first policy address on 22 May 2026, announced a review of the existing energy mix target (40% fossil / 35% nuclear / 25% renewable), signalling that the ministry would advocate for a higher renewable share.

The transition also raised questions about the future of Okaiken's nuclear fleet. The [[Kizan Nuclear Power Plant|Kizan]] and [[Aizawa Nuclear Power Plant|Aizawa]] nuclear power plants — both of which have been the subject of longstanding safety concerns and public opposition — are due for licence renewal reviews in 2027 and 2028 respectively. The ministry's position on these renewals is expected to be a major political issue in the coming years.

==Controversies==

===Geothermal energy vs. onsen preservation===
The most persistent and politically charged controversy facing the Ministry of the Environment is the conflict between [[geothermal energy]] development and the preservation of [[onsen|hot spring]] resources. Okaiken possesses the third-largest geothermal potential of any country, estimated at 23 GW, but currently exploits only approximately 600 MW — roughly 0.5% of its geothermal resource base. The primary obstacle to expanded geothermal development is the opposition of the onsen industry, which generates approximately {{currency|210 billion|SEN}} in annual tourism revenue and employs an estimated 150,000 people.

Onsen operators contend that geothermal drilling can alter the hydrology of hot spring systems, reducing water temperatures and flow rates, and that the industrial character of geothermal plants is incompatible with the cultural and aesthetic values of onsen tourism. The [[Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act (Okaiken)|Geothermal-Onsen Coexistence Act]] of 1994 established a mediation framework, but disputes have continued. In 2023, a proposed 50 MW geothermal project in [[Kiso Prefecture]] was abandoned after a two-year mediation process failed to resolve objections from 14 onsen operators, who argued that the project would threaten the {{Nihongo|"Thousand-Year Spring"|千年の湯|Sennen no Yu}} — a historically significant onsen dating to the 12th century.

The ministry has been criticised from both sides of the debate. Environmental groups and energy policy analysts accuse the ministry of being too deferential to onsen interests, arguing that the Coexistence Act's buffer zone requirements and mediation procedures effectively give onsen operators a veto over geothermal development. Onsen industry groups, conversely, argue that the ministry has not done enough to enforce the Act's protective provisions and that its advocacy for geothermal expansion reflects a bias toward industrial energy interests over cultural preservation.

The [[En Crisis (2026)|En Crisis]] reignited the debate, with proponents of geothermal energy arguing that domestic renewable sources are essential for energy security, while onsen advocates warned that hasty development could inflict irreversible damage on a cultural and economic asset worth far more than the energy savings.

===Nuclear waste management===
The ministry shares responsibility for [[nuclear waste]] management with the [[Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry (Okaiken)|Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry]] (METI) and the [[Nuclear Regulation Authority (Okaiken)|Nuclear Regulation Authority]]. Okaiken's four nuclear power plants — [[Kizan Nuclear Power Plant|Kizan]], [[Aizawa Nuclear Power Plant|Aizawa]], [[Tokuyama Nuclear Power Plant|Tokuyama]], and [[Shiga Nuclear Power Plant|Shiga]] — collectively produce approximately 900 tonnes of [[spent nuclear fuel]] annually, for which no long-term disposal solution has been implemented.

The ministry's 2022 proposal for a [[deep geological repository]] in [[Ishijima Prefecture]] met fierce opposition from local residents and the prefectural government, who argued that the site selection process was opaque and that the geological suitability of the proposed location had not been adequately demonstrated. The proposal was formally withdrawn in March 2024, and the ministry has since been conducting a nationwide survey of potential repository sites — a process that is expected to take at least a decade.

The controversy is compounded by the political sensitivity of the Kizan and Aizawa plants, which have been the subject of safety concerns since the 2011 [[Fukushima Daiichi nuclear disaster|Fukushima]] accident prompted a nationwide review of nuclear safety standards. The ministry has advocated for the inclusion of nuclear waste management costs in the overall economic assessment of nuclear power, a position that has drawn opposition from METI and the nuclear industry.

===Deep-sea mining and the Shendan Ocean===
The ministry has been involved in a protracted inter-ministerial dispute over the environmental implications of [[deep-sea mining]] in the [[Shendan Ocean]], where the [[Shendan Ocean Mining Authority]] (SOMA) has issued exploration licences for [[polymetallic nodule]] extraction. The ministry's Environmental Impact Assessment Agency has raised concerns about the potential impact of seabed mining on deep-sea ecosystems, including the destruction of [[hydrothermal vent]] communities and the generation of sediment plumes that could affect mid-water and surface ecosystems.

In 2024, the ministry issued a formal objection to SOMA's proposed pilot mining programme, arguing that the environmental impact assessment was insufficient and that the precautionary principle should be applied. METI overruled the objection, citing national strategic interests in securing supplies of [[cobalt]], [[nickel]], and [[manganese]] for battery production. The ministry subsequently petitioned the Cabinet Office for a review of the inter-ministerial consultation process on deep-sea mining, but no action had been taken before the collapse of the Honda-Nakayama Cabinet in March 2026.

===Kajika indigenous fishing rights===
The [[Kajika people|Kajika indigenous people]]'s traditional fishing rights in the [[Ogasawara]] EEZ have been a source of tension between the ministry's conservation objectives and its obligations to indigenous communities. The [[Ogasawara Marine Protection Act (Okaiken)|Ogasawara Marine Protection Act]] of 2008 recognises Kajika traditional fishing practices but subjects them to conservation requirements, which Kajika representatives argue are overly restrictive and fail to account for the sustainability of traditional methods.

In 2025, the Kajika Tribal Council filed a formal complaint with the ministry, alleging that recent restrictions on seasonal fishing grounds — imposed to protect declining [[tuna]] stocks — violated the spirit of the Marine Protection Act's indigenous rights provisions. The ministry established a consultation committee, but the process was disrupted by the political turmoil of early 2026. Minister Yamada has indicated that the Kajika fishing rights issue will be a priority for her tenure.

